# Final General Management Plan and Environmental Impact Statement, Volume 1, Gettysburg National Military Park (National Park Service, 1999; the plan describes 1998 conditions)
# Read as follows: the plan is a management document. Its history chapters and its comparison of the park's own base maps of 1863, 1895, 1927 and 1993 (built from Bachelder 1863 and 1872, Warren 1868,
# period photographs and sketches, PDF p. 59) are the report's account of the 1863 landscape; its alternatives are proposals (state date '~1998 planning prescription', use indirect) and describe nothing in July 1863.
# A statement about the condition of the surviving fences, woods or buildings in 1998 is never an 1863 profile.
# Printed page numbers are the numbers on the page (roman numerals in the foreword); they are given after the PDF page, as 61/33.
# The text layer is a clean machine text with a few damaged letters (bams for barns, Gulp's for Culp's); a quote that contains one of them is written in the cleaned form with the explicit {{saved|fixed}} marks.
# The text page 59, the tabular page 61 and the map page 62 were looked at as page images.
@ NPS-GMP-1999-V1
%images 59, 61, 62

%basis the park historians' base maps: Bachelder 1863 and 1872, Warren 1868 and others, period photographs and sketches, park archives (PDF p. 59)
61/33 | fields | The plan's totals for 1863 and 1993: woodlots and woodlands, orchards, historic fences | - | 1863 (NPS base-map comparison of 1998; the 1993 column is from aerial photography) | stated | Table 1 of the plan gives in its 1863 Landscape and 1993 Landscape columns woodlots and woodlands 898 and 1,974 acres, orchards 230 and 18 acres and historic fences 160 and 43 linear miles; the page does not state the area the table covers (the map page that follows shows the park and the surrounding historic district) | A comparison of 1863 and 1993 conditions for selected landscape elements >>> Historic Fences Linear miles 160 43 | @59: #s14-16
62/35 | general | Figure 3 maps the fences, orchards and woods of 1863 | - | 1863 (NPS base map of 1998) | stated | Figure 3 of the plan, titled Landscape Characteristics 1863, is a base map whose legend names historic features as fences, orchards and woods, over the outlines of the Gettysburg NMP, the Eisenhower NHS and the Gettysburg Historic District; the map page shows fence lines as thin lines, orchards hatched and woods grey (a page image was looked at; no geometry is read from it here) | Figure 3 Landscape Characteristics 1863 >>> Eisenhower NHS Boundary
59/31 | general | How the park's 1863 base map was made | - | ~1998 (method note for the base maps of 1863, 1895, 1927 and 1993) | stated | The plan says park historians mapped the eras 1863, 1895, 1927 and 1993 on base maps at a common scale, from park archival materials, library records, period photographs and sketches, maps (Bachelder 1863 and 1872, Warren 1868 and others) and, for the 20th century, aerial photographs | #s10-16
60/32 | general | Features mapped for each era | - | ~1998 (method note) | stated | The plan lists the features mapped on the base maps as woodlands, woodlots, thickets, orchards, open fields, fences, roads and lanes, railways, waterways and the town of Gettysburg, each verified or corrected with the park's period photographs | Mapped features were verified or corrected >>> and the town of Gettysburg.
60/32 | general | The town of Gettysburg in 1863: about 2,400 residents | - | 1863 | stated | The plan says that the town of Gettysburg on its 1863 map was smaller than today, housing about 2,400 residents | #s8 | #s5
60/32 | woodlots | Thickets at the time of the battle in the area between the Codori and Trostle houses | bld-codori-farm, bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 (thickets at the time of the battle) | stated | The plan names the area between the Codori and Trostle houses as one that was a thicket at the time of the battle and had grown into woodland by the time of its 1895 map | #s11 | #s9
60/32 | general | The 1895 base map: relatively little changed since the battle; orchards had changed | - | ~1895 (War Department period, from the plan's 1895 map) | stated | The plan says that its 1895 map shows relatively little change since the battle, that most of the 1863 fences, road systems, fields and woods remained much as they had been (monuments and avenues of the Union line excepted), and that the location and quantity of orchards had changed | #s9-11
60/32 | fences | The 1927 base map: fewer and smaller orchards; the 1863 field and fence patterns basically unchanged; avenue fencing new | - | ~1927 (end of veteran management, from the plan's 1927 map) | stated | The plan says that its 1927 map shows fewer and smaller orchards, that under the War Department the 1863 field patterns and fence patterns were basically unchanged, and that new fencing separated the designed landscape of the avenues from the historic farms | #s14-16
60/32 | fences | The 1993 base map: most field and farm-boundary fences gone, orchards almost entirely removed, wooded areas expanded | - | ~1993 (condition of 1993 compared with 1863) | stated | The plan says that the 1993 map shows orchards almost entirely removed from the battlefield, wooded areas greatly expanded from their 1863 boundaries, most of the fences that defined field patterns and farm boundaries disappeared, and many 1863 lanes gone | #s21-24
67/44 | orchards | The Peach Orchard today is managed for fruit and bears little resemblance to its state in 1863 | bld-sherfy-farm | ~1998 (condition of 1998) | stated | The plan's caption says the present day Peach Orchard is managed in modern fashion for fruit production and bears little resemblance to its state in 1863 | #s8
67/44 | woodlots | Reynolds Woods: an 1863 photograph and the thicker, undergrown woods of today | - | ~1998 (caption comparing an 1863 photograph with the woods of 1998; the primary document is the Library of Congress photograph) | stated | The plan's caption pairs a photograph of Reynolds Woods from McPherson Ridge in 1863 with the remark that the woods are now thicker and choked with undergrowth, so that it is difficult to understand how troops could have moved through this area in battle | Above right: Reynolds Woods from McPherson Ridge, 1863. >>> through this area in battle.
68/45 | woodlots | The Herbst woodlot an avenue of approach (open quality significant); Patterson woods used for concealment only | bld-herbst | 1863-07 (the park historians' analysis of what the woodlots did in the battle) | stated | The plan says park historians found the Herbst woodlot was among other things an avenue of approach for troops, so that its open quality contributed to its use in the battle, and Patterson woods was used for concealment only, so that its size and boundary matter but not its openness | #s9-10
87/67 | woodlots | Under the 1982 plan the park cut and opened Little Round Top, Houck's Ridge and areas of the Rose Farm | bld-rose-farm | ~1980s (cutting under the 1982 general management plan, not the state of 1863) | stated | The plan says the 1982 plan called for removing woodlands that were not part of the 1863 landscape, replacing the historic orchards and managing 11 historic woodlots to 1863 conditions, that under it the park cut and opened Little Round Top, Houck's Ridge and areas of the Rose Farm, and that little else of this was done | #s4-6
23/xxiii | woodlots | Under the Memorial Association's policy of leaving the land untouched, woods grew up on the west slope of Little Round Top and dense growth overtook woodlots | - | ~1864-1880 (growth after the battle; the Association's purpose was to keep the points in the condition in which they were left in July 1863) | stated | The plan says the decision of the GBMA (the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association) to leave the land untouched meant that woods grew up on the west slope of Little Round Top, bullet-ridden trees died on Culp's Hill, dense forest growth overtook woodlots, and the breastworks and lunettes on Culp's and Cemetery Hills decayed | =Their decision to leave the land untouched meant that woods grew up on the west slope of Little Round Top, bullet ridden trees died on {{Gulp's|Culp's}} Hill, and dense forest growth overtook woodlots. The Union breastworks and lunettes on {{Gulp's|Culp's}} and Cemetery Hills decayed and fell into disrepair. | #s4-7
23/xxiii | earthworks | The GBMA rebuilt defenses after 1880 in order to retain the 1863 appearance | - | ~1880-1895 (defenses rebuilt by the GBMA) | stated | The plan says that after veterans of the Grand Army of the Republic bought a majority of the GBMA stock in 1880 the GBMA rebuilt defenses and actively maintained the natural features of its holdings in order to retain the 1863 appearance (a rebuilt work is not an 1863 profile) | #s12-14
23/xxiii | monuments | GBMA policy: first monument in the line of battle, secondary markers after | - | !1880-1895 (GBMA after 1880) | stated | The plan says the GBMA required regiments and batteries to place their first monument in their line of battle and permitted secondary position markers afterwards, and that veterans erected several hundred monuments, memorials and markers | #s15
23/xxiii | general | Little changed on the battlefield in the 20 years after the battle: fences repaired, most buildings reconstructed; Bachelder in 1873 and 1890 | - | ~1863-1890 (statement of 1999 on the first 20 years after the battle) | stated | The plan says that beyond the repair of fences and the reconstruction of most buildings the area changed very little in the 20 years after the battle, that John Bachelder observed in 1873 that no material changes had been made in the field, and that by 1890 the only significant change he noted was tree growth obscuring points and views that had not existed in 1863 | #s19-21 | @24: farming practices and population changed little, the pattern of buildings, fields, woodlots and fence lines remained largely as it had been in 1863.
24/xxiv | monuments | Cannon placed on the field by the Commission | - | !1893-1913 (Commission and War Department period) | stated | The plan says that more than 400 surplus cannon, some of which saw action in the 1863 battle, were placed on the field in the locations from which they were engaged | #s16
24/xxiv | later | Bridges, copings, observation towers, shell stones and fencing designed by Cope were imposed on a small part of the battlefield | - | !1893-1913 (the two decades after the Commission of 1893) | stated | The plan says that over the two decades after the 1893 commission it added specially designed bridges, copings, observation towers, shell stones and fencing designed by the park engineer Emmor B. Cope, imposed only on the avenues and the areas around the monuments, a newer designed part of the park separate from the older rural landscape of the field of battle | #s18-20 | #s5
26/xxvi | general | War Department period: the Commission kept 1863 topography, woodlots, reforested areas, field sizes and fence lines | - | ~1893-1927 (War Department period, statement of 1999) | stated | The plan says the Commission made preservation of existing topography and 1863 features a major principle, maintained woodlots in their historic condition, reforested areas that were wooded during the battle but where trees had died or been cut, and maintained historic field sizes, fence lines, patterns and farm boundaries | #s1-3
26/xxvi | buildings | On the farms a few buildings changed after the battle: burned barns replaced, outbuildings added, orchards expanded or moved | - | ~1893-1927 (War Department period; changes since the battle) | stated | The plan says that on the farms a few buildings might have changed, burned barns might have been replaced by new structures, outbuildings added and orchards expanded or moved, while the Commission kept the general arrangement of a building complex as it was in 1863 | =On these farms, as on privately-held battlefield land, a few buildings might have changed, burned {{bams|barns}} might have been replaced by new structures, outbuildings added, and orchards expanded or moved. However, the Commission ensured that the general arrangement and spatial qualities of a building complex continued to reflect the 1863 conditions.
26/xxvi | later | After 1927 the managers widened and straightened avenues and removed Cope's fences and stone details | - | !1927-1930s (after the death of the park engineer Cope in 1927) | stated | The plan says that within a month of Cope's death in 1927 the new managers began widening and straightening avenues for automobile traffic and removing the fences and stone details that characterized his design, and began modernizing agricultural practices | #s12-14
26/xxvi | later | The park of 1933: 2,530 acres, 23.2 miles of avenues, 1,728 monuments and commemorative structures, 18 farms | - | !1933 (transfer to the National Park Service) | stated | The plan says the park when transferred to the NPS in 1933 included 2,530 acres, 23.2 miles of avenues, 1,728 monuments and commemorative structures and 18 farms | In 1933, the park was transferred to the administration >>> and 18 farms.
26/xxvi | fences | From the 1930s the Park Service removed historic fences, combined fields, drained wetlands and removed boulders; woods grew up on land not useful for farming | - | ~1933-1990s (National Park Service era) | stated | The plan says that NPS began an active campaign to remove historic fences, combine fields, drain wetlands and remove boulders, allowed woods to grow up in areas not useful for modern agriculture, and changed plowing patterns, so that the 1863 pattern of lanes, fields and woodlands is obscured | #s23-27
22/xxii | hospitals | The area affected by the battle, with its network of field hospitals, covered more than 22,000 acres | - | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says the area affected by the conflict, including the vast network of military field hospitals, covered more than 22,000 acres, and that tens of thousands of wounded men lay scattered in farm fields and private homes within little more than a week | #s10-11
22/xxii | damage | The armies left thousands of acres of trampled crops, miles of dismantled fencing and burned buildings | - | 1863-07 to 1863-08 | stated | The plan says that as many as 200,000 men and horses had lived off the land and left behind thousands of acres of trampled crops, miles of dismantled and destroyed fencing and burned buildings, and that the dead were buried throughout the battlefield near where they fell or near field hospitals | #s12-13
40/12 | woodlots | Boundaries of wooded areas were not kept at their historical limits; battle views and avenues of approach obscured | - | ~1930s-1990s (condition of 1998 compared with the battle) | stated | The plan says managers made little attempt to keep the boundaries of wooded areas at their historical limits, so that the pattern of open and wooded terrain that determined where the armies moved was lost or obscured, and critical views and avenues of approach are obscured at Culp's Hill and between Warfield Ridge and Little Round Top | #s2-4
40/12 | fields | Modern farming modified or removed field topography, lanes, fence lines, wetlands, orchards and farm boundaries of 1863 | - | ~1930s-1990s (condition of 1998 compared with the battle) | stated | The plan says that many features of the 1863 battle landscape, including field topography, historic lanes, fence lines, wetlands and watercourses, orchards and farm boundaries, were modified or eliminated to increase field sizes for modern machinery | #s9
81/61 | buildings | The Codori barn did not exist in 1863 but stands on the site of the original 1863 structure | bld-codori-farm | !after 1863 (the present barn; the plan says it did not exist in 1863) | stated | The plan gives the Codori barn as the example of a building that did not exist in 1863 but is on the site of the original 1863 structure | #s15
141/129 | buildings | Bliss Farm buildings destroyed during the battle and never rebuilt | bld-bliss-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says that buildings important to the battle are not now visible on the field because, as with the Bliss Farm, they were destroyed during the battle and never rebuilt, or were lost since to fire, storm damage or demolition | #s9-11
174/168 | buildings | Many buildings of 1863 remain, some modernized, damaged or lost to fire or storms | - | ~1998 (condition of 1998 of the buildings of 1863) | stated | The plan says that while many of the buildings and structures present in 1863 remain, some of them have been modernized, damaged or lost to fire or storms | #s12
174/168 | woodlots | Areas that were thickets during the battle are mature woodlands today; unused fields have become wooded | - | ~1998 (condition of 1998 compared with the battle) | stated | The plan says natural processes have changed many battle features, so that areas that were thickets during the battle are mature woodlands today and unused farm fields have become wooded | #s13-14
175/169 | buildings | At least 70 NPS-owned historic buildings were present during the 1863 conflict | - | pre-1863 to 1863-07 (count of surviving buildings of 1863 as of 1998; some enlarged or altered since) | stated | The plan says NPS owns 100 of the 148 historic buildings in the park boundary, at least 70 of which were present during the 1863 conflict although some have been enlarged or altered, and that post-Civil War historic buildings augment these original farm buildings | #s13-15
175/169 | roads | Farm lanes enclosed on both edges by fencing were useful as defensive positions in the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says farm lanes provided access for the armies and that where they were enclosed on both edges by fencing they became useful locations for defensive positions | #s23
175/169 | roads | The 1863 alignment of roads and lanes has remained generally intact although surfaces changed | - | ~1998 (condition of 1998; the alignment is stated to be that of 1863) | stated | The plan says that although the surface materials of the roads and lanes have changed and some grading has occurred, the 1863 alignment of these features has remained generally intact | #s24
176/170 | earthworks | Stone and earthen defense works of the battle: most rebuilt by the GBMA or War Department in the late 19th century | - | ~1880s-1900s (rebuilt works; the plan calls the original works the only structural artifacts left behind from the battle) | stated | The plan says the stone and earthen defense works created by battle participants are the only structural artifacts left from the battle and that most of them were rebuilt by the GBMA or the War Department in the late 19th century and are now part of the formal monumentation of the park | Other structures include the stone and earthen defense works >>> part of the formal monumentation of the park.
176/170 | fences | Field and farm boundaries were once delineated by timber rail and stone fencing | - | 1863 and earlier (the plan writes that the diversity of farms was once obvious in this delineation; no date is given) | stated | The plan says the diversity of agricultural use and of individual farms was once obvious in the delineation of field and farm boundaries by timber rail and stone fencing | #s3
176/170 | fences | The vast majority of the stone walls around farm fields survived the battle; less than one third of the timber rail fences present before the battle remain | - | ~1998 (condition of 1998 compared with the fences before the battle) | stated | The plan says the vast majority of the stone walls that enclosed the farm fields have survived the battle, that many timber rail fences have been removed, and that less than one third of the fences present before the battle remain on the battlefield today | #s4-5
176/170 | monuments | First monuments built in the 1870s by Grand Army of the Republic posts; most monuments erected 1885-1915; the Peace Memorial 1938 | - | !1870s-1938 | stated | The plan says Grand Army of the Republic posts built the first monuments in the 1870s to mark where Union officers fell, most monuments were erected in a thirty-year period (1885-1915), cannon on cast-iron carriages flank the battery monuments, and the commemorative era culminated in 1938 with the Eternal Light Peace Memorial | #s9-12
176/170 | avenues | The park avenues follow the battle lines and total almost 35 miles | - | !1890s-1930s (avenues built by the Commission and War Department; length as stated for 1998) | stated | The plan says the system of park avenues follows the battle lines of the contending armies and totals almost 35 miles, with the alignment of avenues, monuments and mounted cannon still intact although a small percentage has been realigned | #s15-16
179/173 | buildings | 71 farms associated with the 1863 battlefield lie within the park boundary | - | 1863 (farms of the 1863 battlefield counted within the 1998 boundary) | stated | The plan says that the park boundary includes 71 farms that were associated with the 1863 battlefield, into which thirty-seven 18th century farms have been incorporated | #s9
179/173 | buildings | Civil War-era farms with only ruins or no surface remains: Rose barn ruins; Tawney, Wentz and G. W. Weikert remains; Poor Farm, Granite Schoolhouse, Forney, Heagy, Rogers, Staub, Guinn and Fisher farms | bld-rose-farm, bld-wentz-farm, bld-rogers-house | pre-1863 to 1863-07 (farms of the battle era; the remains are as of 1998) | stated | The plan names Civil War-era farms whose buildings no longer stand: the ruins of the Rose barn and building remains at the Z. Tawney, J. Tawney, Wentz and G. W. Weikert farms, and farms with no surface remains, among them the extensive complex of the Adams County Poor Farm (hospitals, asylums, workhouses, dwellings and barns), the Granite Schoolhouse and the Forney, Heagy, Rogers, Staub, Guinn and Fisher farms | #s15-18
179/173 | buildings | The Bliss Farm was burned on the third day of the battle and never rebuilt as a home site | bld-bliss-farm | 1863-07-03 | stated | The plan says the Bliss Farm was burned on the third day of the battle and abandoned ever after as a home site | Perhaps most notable of those farms with no above-ground building resources >>> abandoned ever after as a home site.
179/173 | hospitals | Original interment sites, encampment sites and hospital sites: July 1863 military use extended to almost all corners of the park | - | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says the park's archeological resources include original interment sites, encampment sites and hospital sites, because military use in July 1863 extended to almost all corners of the park, so that evidence of encampments, hospitals and interments can occur almost anywhere within the boundaries | #s23-24
180/174 | fields | Field sizes have increased since the time of the battle (modern farming, 1998) | - | ~1998 (condition of 1998 compared with the time of the battle) | stated | The plan says that to accommodate modern agriculture field sizes have increased compared with agricultural uses at the time of the battle, and that crops and cattle herds are larger or of modern appearance | #s20
180/174 | fences | 17 miles of present fencing stand on sites of fences present in 1863 and enclose pastures | - | ~1998 (modern fences of 1998 on the sites of 1863 fences; not an 1863 profile) | stated | The plan says the park contains 17 miles of fencing that occupy the sites of fences present in 1863 and are used to enclose pastures, kept up from the proceeds of the agriculture program | #s23-24
184/178 | general | Wet areas that existed during the 1863 battle were later drained; the 1868 Warren survey noted some larger swampy areas | - | 1863-07 (wet areas) and 1868 (Warren survey) | stated | The plan says no inventory of historic wetlands exists, that the 1868 Warren survey noted some of the larger swampy areas, that later landowners and farmers drained historic wetlands and wet areas that existed during the 1863 battle, and that the extent of field drains gives some indication of their historic extent | #s21-23
185/179 | woodlots | The original forest of the Gettysburg area was cleared by 18th and 19th century settlers | - | pre-1863 | stated | The plan says the original forest that covered most of the Gettysburg area disappeared as 18th and 19th century settlers cleared areas for farming and used forests for fuel and building | The original forest that covered most of the Gettysburg area disappeared >>> for fuel and building.
126/111 | woodlots | Plan alternative B (Major Battle Action Area): remove 576 acres of woodlands not present in 1863, add 115 acres present in 1863, restore 9.8 miles of lanes and woodlot roads | - | ~1998 (planning prescription measured against the park's 1863 base map; not a state of 1863) | stated | The plan's alternative B prescribes for the Major Battle Action Area removing 576 acres of woodlands not present in 1863, adding 115 acres of woodlands present in 1863, and restoring 9.8 miles of historic lanes and woodlot roads | #s2-4 | Land and Resources Major Battle Action Area
136/124 | fences | Plan alternative C (Major Battle Action Area): reconstruct 39.1 miles of missing fencing, manage 278 acres of woodlots, develop 160 acres of orchards, maintain 65 acres of thickets | - | ~1998 (planning prescription measured against the park's 1863 base map; not a state of 1863) | stated | The plan's alternative C prescribes removing 576 acres of nonhistoric woodlands and adding 115 acres present in 1863, reconstructing 39.1 miles of missing fencing, managing 278 acres of woodlands as woodlots, developing 160 acres of orchards and maintaining 65 acres of thickets at their historic height | #s12-16 | Land and Resources Major Battle Action Area
149/140 | fences | Plan alternative D (whole park): remove 1,015 acres of non-historic woods, reconstruct 104 miles of fences, restore 16 miles of historic lanes, manage 321 acres of woodlots | - | ~1998 (planning prescription measured against the park's 1863 base map; not a state of 1863) | stated | The plan's alternative D prescribes removing 1,015 acres of non-historic woods, reconstructing 104 miles of fences, restoring or reconstructing 16 miles of historic lanes and managing 321 acres of woodland as woodlots | #s11-16
151/142 | orchards | Plan alternative D (other resource areas): add 104 miles of 1863 fencing, remove 1,015 acres of woodlands, plant 221 acres of orchards | - | ~1998 (planning prescription; not a state of 1863) | stated | The plan's alternative D lists as the types of actions adding 104 miles of 1863 fencing, removing 1,015 acres of woodlands and planting 221 acres of orchards | #s8-10
